WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.760
Agent systems need a clear identity model.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:12.020
A request may involve a human user, an application service account, delegated access to another system, a workload identity, or a tool credential.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:21.200
The design must answer whether an action runs as the user, as the application, as a specific service account, or as a constrained delegation of user authority.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:29.020
Without that clarity, audit trails become confusing and permissions tend to expand until the agent can do far more than the workflow requires.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.840
Authorization should happen before retrieval and before tool use.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.700
If a user is not allowed to read a document, the agent should not retrieve it into context.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.080
If a user is not allowed to update a record, the model should not be able to reach a tool that updates it on the user's behalf.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:54.300
Object-level access control, tenant boundaries, organization membership, role checks, and resource ownership should be enforced in Python code or trusted services outside the model.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:01:01.080
Least privilege means the agent receives only the permissions, tools, data, and credentials needed for its approved task.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:10.660
Broad credentials may feel flexible during development, but they enlarge the blast radius of misconfiguration, prompt injection, model error, or operator mistake.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:21.360
Scoped credentials, permission narrowing, destination controls, short-lived access, read-only modes, and default-deny behavior help keep agent authority aligned with business intent.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:27.820
Every sensitive action should verify subject, action, resource, and context outside the model.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.640
Who is requesting the action?

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.700
What action is being requested?

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.520
Which resource is affected?

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.140
Which tenant or organization owns it?

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.120
What policy applies now?

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.020
What approval is required?

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.640
The model can draft or recommend, but the application should enforce these decisions.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:48.140
That separation is the backbone of safe agent automation.
